<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:local="clr-namespace:LamSims.App"
        x:Class="LamSims.App.MainWindow"
        Width="900" Height="640"
        MinWidth="760" MinHeight="560"
        Title="LamSims Updater">
  <DockPanel Margin="12">

    <!-- While shutdown waits for the runner the content stays visible and goes
         inert. Bound on the three content blocks rather than on the DockPanel so the stopping
         notice below is not dimmed along with the controls it explains. -->
    <!-- The region is bounded as a WHOLE, and this is the outermost of three nested scrolls
         rather than a replacement for the two inside it. Those bound the two lists that GROW
         without limit - the unlocker's targets, the banner run - and they are what keeps the game
         folder and the catalog box in place when those lists get long. Neither bounds the region:
         a section's FIXED parts (two button rows, the detection notes, the progress row) sit
         outside both caps, so one expanded section still took the window.

         Measured, not chosen. With the DLC Unlocker open the region wants 429px whatever the
         window height, and it is docked to the top of a DockPanel, so the pack list and the log
         divide what is left: 69px each at the 560 minimum and 149 at the 640 default, against the
         100px floor the layout tests hold the log to. A literal MaxHeight cannot fix that - below
         429 it clips a section even at 1000px where there is room, above it, it is no cap at all
         at 560. So the cap is a share of the window and moves with it: 0.62 leaves the content
         213px at 560 and 243 at 640, and stops binding entirely past about 690px, where the region
         fits unaided and this scroller never appears.

         AllowAutoHide is off for the same reason it is off on the target list: Fluent OVERLAYS a
         scrollbar that can hide, and here it would land on the Browse buttons down the right edge. -->
    <ScrollViewer DockPanel.Dock="Top" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                  HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled" AllowAutoHide="False"
                  MaxHeight="{Binding $parent[Window].Bounds.Height,
                              Converter={x:Static local:Ratio.Of}, ConverterParameter=0.62}">
    <StackPanel Spacing="6" IsEnabled="{Binding !IsShuttingDown}">
      <!-- 128, and the same 128 in all five label grids below, which is what keeps the labels in
           one column down the window. Measured under Skia rather than chosen: the longest label,
           "Catalog (in use):", wants 109px in the bundled Inter, and the 110 this used to be left it
           one pixel from clipping into the value beside it — a Grid does not clip its children, so
           it would have overlapped rather than truncated. Headless cannot check this: it stubs the
           text shaper and measures every glyph to the same width, so a width assertion there says
           nothing about the real face. WindowLabelColumnTests guards the agreement instead. -->
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="128,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Game folder:" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding GameDirectory}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
        <Button Grid.Column="2" Content="Browse" Command="{Binding BrowseGameCommand}" />
      </Grid>

      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="128,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8" RowSpacing="2">
        <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Text="Catalog:" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBox Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding CatalogInput}"
                 PlaceholderText="A catalog.json path, or an https:// URL" />
        <StackPanel Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
          <Button Content="Load" Command="{Binding ApplyCatalogSourceCommand}" />
          <Button Content="Browse" Command="{Binding ChangeCatalogCommand}" />
          <Button Content="Use cached copy" Command="{Binding UseCachedCatalogCommand}" />
        </StackPanel>
        <!-- The source actually in use, which differs from the box above whenever the box holds
             an edit the user has not pressed Load on yet. Labelled, because unlabelled it is a
             grey path under a text box that holds a different path, and a reader has no way to
             tell which of the two the application is reading. Both halves hide together: with
             nothing loaded the description is empty, and a lone "In use:" beside nothing at all
             says less than a row that is not there. -->
        <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Text="Catalog (in use):" Classes="secondary"
                   IsVisible="{Binding CatalogDescription,
                               Converter={x:Static StringConverters.IsNotNullOrEmpty}}" />
        <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding CatalogDescription}" Classes="secondary"
                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                   IsVisible="{Binding CatalogDescription,
                               Converter={x:Static StringConverters.IsNotNullOrEmpty}}" />
      </Grid>

      <Expander x:Name="AdvancedSection" Header="Advanced">
        <StackPanel Spacing="6">
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="128,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Downloads:" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding DownloadDirectory}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
            <Button Grid.Column="2" Content="Browse" Command="{Binding BrowseDownloadsCommand}" />
          </Grid>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="128,Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Connections:" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            <Slider Grid.Column="1" Minimum="1" Maximum="16" Width="220"
                    Value="{Binding Connections}" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="{Binding Connections}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Grid>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="128,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Wine prefix:" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding WinePrefix}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
            <Button Grid.Column="2" Content="Browse" Command="{Binding BrowseWinePrefixCommand}" />
          </Grid>
        </StackPanel>
      </Expander>

      <!-- Hidden only when no backend reports support: the EA-client backend reports for
           Windows and the Wine backend for Linux, so this shows on either. On any other
           platform, including macOS, neither backend reports supported and this stays hidden
           rather than offering a control that cannot work.
           The nested DataContext is why the binding sweep has to re-root; without that, every
           binding below would read as unresolved. -->
      <Expander x:Name="UnlockerSection" Header="DLC Unlocker" DataContext="{Binding Unlocker}"
                IsVisible="{Binding IsSupported}">
        <StackPanel Spacing="6">
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="8">
            <Button Grid.Column="0" Content="Select all"
                    Command="{Binding SelectAllCommand}" />
            <Button Grid.Column="1" Content="Select none"
                    Command="{Binding SelectNoneCommand}" />
            <Button Grid.Column="2" HorizontalAlignment="Right" Content="Refresh"
                    Command="{Binding RefreshCommand}" />
          </Grid>
          <!-- OUTSIDE the scroller below, deliberately. These are the only three things detection
               reports that ask something of the user, and inside the target list's scroller they
               scrolled out of sight behind eight rows nobody had to read.

               The scroll lives HERE, inside the section that grows, and not around the settings
               region as a whole: wrapped outside, unfolding a section made the game folder, the
               catalog box and the Scan row scroll away with it, which is worse than the overflow it
               was fixing. The target list is the only part of this section whose height is not
               fixed — one row per client per prefix, and a machine with several launchers has
               plenty. Select all / Refresh sit above this and Install / Remove below it, outside the
               scroller, so the controls are reachable whatever the list does.

               140 is measured, not picked: it is four target rows, and it fixes the section's height
               so eight targets and fifteen arrange identically. Three targets — the reported case —
               sit under the cap, so nothing scrolls and the pack list keeps 149px. Past the cap the
               pack list holds 71px at the 640px default and 331px at 900. Raising the cap buys
               target rows straight out of the pack list: at 180 the list was 31px, which reads as
               broken.

               AllowAutoHide is off because Fluent's scrollbar OVERLAYS the content when it can hide,
               and it landed on top of the status column: "Not install" with the bar over the rest.
               Off, it reserves its own width. HorizontalScrollBarVisibility is off explicitly too:
               enabled, it measures the content with infinite width and the TextTrimming on every
               path below stops firing. -->
          <ScrollViewer MaxHeight="140" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                        HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled" AllowAutoHide="False">
          <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Targets}">
            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate>
                <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                  <CheckBox Grid.Column="0" IsChecked="{Binding IsSelected}"
                            IsEnabled="{Binding !IsBusy}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  <StackPanel Grid.Column="1">
                    <!-- Title, not DisplayName: one flat property so the binding sweep can
                         resolve it, and it is where the environment appears off Windows. -->
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Title}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding ClientPath}" Classes="secondary"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                    <!-- Per row rather than a banner: banners key by id, so a batch's warnings
                         would collapse to whichever target reported last. -->
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Warning}" Classes="secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               IsVisible="{Binding Warning, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                  </StackPanel>
                  <!-- Wrapped, not trimmed: a failed operation puts its error message here, and
                       those interpolate paths and exception messages. There is no banner left to
                       carry them and no tooltip anywhere in this window, so trimming to the 220
                       cap would show the user "Instal…" and nothing else. The pack list wraps its
                       own status for the same reason. -->
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="{Binding StatusText}" MaxWidth="220"
                             TextWrapping="Wrap" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </Grid>
              </DataTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
          </ItemsControl>
          </ScrollViewer>
          <!-- The three things detection can report that ask something of the user: a prefix they
               named in the setting that is not one, a Flatpak sandbox that cannot see the host, and
               finding nothing at all. Every other diagnostic — a prefix with no client registered, a
               32-bit prefix, an unreadable launcher config — goes to the log, because one line per
               prefix examined is a list nobody reads and it used to bury the buttons below.
               The `secondary` class already exists and is styled; a new class here would fail
               ThemeSweepTests in both directions. -->
          <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding DetectionNotes}"
                        IsVisible="{Binding HasDetectionNotes}">
            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate>
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding}" Classes="secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
              </DataTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
          </ItemsControl>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="8">
            <Button Grid.Column="0" Content="Install selected"
                    Command="{Binding InstallSelectedCommand}" />
            <Button Grid.Column="1" Content="Remove selected"
                    Command="{Binding RemoveSelectedCommand}" />
          </Grid>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8"
                IsVisible="{Binding IsBusy}">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding BatchPosition}" Classes="secondary"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding CurrentStep}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            <!-- Total is bound, not just Completed: the step count each operation publishes is
                 only visible to a user if the bar knows where the end is. -->
            <ProgressBar Grid.Column="2" Width="120" Minimum="0"
                         Maximum="{Binding Total}" Value="{Binding Completed}"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            <!-- The denominator, not just the numerator: Total already reaches the bar's fill,
                 but without this the text beside it shows "7" where the design shows "7/10". -->
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="3" VerticalAlignment="Center" Text="{Binding Completed}" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="4" VerticalAlignment="Center" Text="/" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="5" VerticalAlignment="Center" Text="{Binding Total}" />
          </Grid>
          <Button Content="Restart as administrator"
                  Command="{Binding RelaunchElevatedCommand}"
                  IsVisible="{Binding RequiresElevation}" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Expander>

      <!-- Bounded for the same reason the target list is, and in the same shape: the thing that
           grows carries its own scroll, rather than the settings region scrolling as a whole and
           taking the game folder and the catalog box out of reach with it. A run of banners is
           rarer than a long target list but no more bounded — one per failure, each dismissible. -->
      <ScrollViewer MaxHeight="120" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                    HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
      <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Banners}">
        <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
          <DataTemplate>
            <Border Classes="banner"
                    Classes.warning="{Binding IsWarning}"
                    Classes.error="{Binding IsError}">
              <!-- Two columns rather than a horizontal StackPanel. A StackPanel measures
                   horizontally with infinite available width, so Stretch is inert and Wrap never
                   fires: the text lays out on one unbroken line and Dismiss (the only route to
                   clear a banner) is arranged off the window, with no horizontal scroll to
                   reach it. Banner text interpolates paths and exception messages. -->
              <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding Text}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <Button Grid.Column="1" Content="Dismiss"
                        Command="{Binding $parent[ItemsControl].DataContext.DismissBannerCommand}"
                        CommandParameter="{Binding}" />
              </Grid>
            </Border>
          </DataTemplate>
        </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
      </ItemsControl>
      </ScrollViewer>

      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
        <Button Grid.Column="0" Content="Select all" Command="{Binding SelectAllCommand}" />
        <Button Grid.Column="1" Content="None" Command="{Binding SelectNoneCommand}" />
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="Search:" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBox Grid.Column="3" MinWidth="160" Text="{Binding SearchText}" />
        <Button Grid.Column="4" Content="Scan" Command="{Binding ScanCommand}" />
      </Grid>
    </StackPanel>
    </ScrollViewer>

    <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8" Margin="0,8,0,0"
          IsEnabled="{Binding !IsShuttingDown}">
      <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding StatusText}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      <Button Grid.Column="1" Content="Pause" Command="{Binding PauseCommand}" />
      <Button Grid.Column="2" Content="Resume" Command="{Binding ResumeCommand}" />
      <Button Grid.Column="3" Content="Cancel all" Command="{Binding CancelAllCommand}" />
      <Button Grid.Column="4" Content="Add selected" Command="{Binding AddSelectedCommand}" />
    </Grid>

    <!-- The stopping state: the content stays visible and goes inert rather than being
         replaced, because shutdown waits for the runner and can take minutes on a large extract.
         A window that emptied itself would look like a crash. -->
    <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Margin="0,8,0,0"
               Text="Finishing up — waiting for the current pack to stop…"
               IsVisible="{Binding IsShuttingDown}" />

    <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,8,2*" IsEnabled="{Binding !IsShuttingDown}">
      <Panel Grid.Column="0">
      <ListBox ItemsSource="{Binding FilteredRows}">
        <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
          <DataTemplate>
            <StackPanel Spacing="2">
              <!-- On the TEMPLATE ROOT, not on the ListBox. Compiled bindings are off in this
                   project, so a ContextMenu attached to the ListBox binds against the inherited
                   MainViewModel, where none of these three commands exist. It resolves to null
                   at runtime with no build error and no exception, and every item is dead. Inside
                   the template it inherits the row's DataContext. -->
              <StackPanel.ContextMenu>
                <ContextMenu>
                  <MenuItem Header="Cancel" Command="{Binding CancelCommand}" />
                  <MenuItem Header="Remove" Command="{Binding RemoveCommand}" />
                  <!-- An Installed row's checkbox is disabled, and this is the only way
                       to re-check it. -->
                  <MenuItem Header="Reinstall anyway" Command="{Binding ReinstallCommand}" />
                </ContextMenu>
              </StackPanel.ContextMenu>
              <!-- Two lines rather than one. Everything on a single line needs the width of a
                   code, a name, a status, a size and a bar side by side, and that width is what
                   the list column has to be given; stacked, the column narrows to the longest
                   pack name and the log gets the rest of the window. -->
              <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto">
                <!-- Negative vertical margin, deliberately. Fluent's CheckBox template pins the
                     grid holding the box to Height="32" — a literal, not a resource — around a box
                     that is 20 and centred, so every row carried 12px of dead height it could not
                     be styled out of. -6 top and bottom gives that 12 back and leaves the box
                     where it already was. -->
                <CheckBox Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Margin="0,-6,8,-6"
                          IsChecked="{Binding IsChecked}" IsEnabled="{Binding IsCheckable}" />
                <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Name}" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2" Classes="secondary" Text="{Binding SizeText}"
                           MinWidth="72"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <!-- A Grid, not a horizontal StackPanel: a StackPanel measures with infinite
                     available width, so the status would never wrap and a Blocked row's error
                     text would lay out past the edge of the column. -->
                <Grid Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                      ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="8">
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="secondary" Text="{Binding Code}"
                             MinWidth="48" />
                  <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Classes="secondary" Text="{Binding StatusText}"
                             TextWrapping="Wrap"
                             Classes.status-ok="{Binding IsStatusOk}"
                             Classes.status-busy="{Binding IsStatusBusy}"
                             Classes.status-warning="{Binding IsStatusWarning}"
                             Classes.status-error="{Binding IsStatusError}" />
                </Grid>
              </Grid>
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Message}" Margin="28,0,0,0" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Classes.warning="{Binding IsWarning}"
                         Classes.error="{Binding IsError}"
                         IsVisible="{Binding Message,
                                     Converter={x:Static StringConverters.IsNotNullOrEmpty}}" />
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding CurrentEntry}" Margin="28,0,0,0" Classes="secondary"
                         TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                         IsVisible="{Binding CurrentEntry,
                                     Converter={x:Static StringConverters.IsNotNullOrEmpty}}" />
              <!-- A rule along the bottom of the row rather than a 120px bar beside the status.
                   The bar was the widest fixed thing in the row and the percentage it showed is
                   already spelled out in StatusText. -->
              <ProgressBar Height="2" MinHeight="0" Minimum="0" Maximum="100"
                           Value="{Binding ProgressPercent}"
                           IsVisible="{Binding IsProgressVisible}" />
            </StackPanel>
          </DataTemplate>
        </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
      </ListBox>

      <!-- LAST child, deliberately. A Panel draws in document order and the ListBox carries an
           opaque MantleBrush background, so a label declared before the list is painted and then
           covered. Nothing binds the list's IsVisible, so this overlays rather than hides. -->
      <TextBlock x:Name="EmptyState"
                 Text="{Binding EmptyStateMessage}"
                 HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                 IsVisible="{Binding EmptyStateMessage,
                             Converter={x:Static StringConverters.IsNotNullOrEmpty}}" />
      </Panel>

      <Border Grid.Column="2" Classes="log" DataContext="{Binding Log}">
        <DockPanel>
          <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                      Spacing="6" Margin="0,6,0,0">
            <Button Content="Copy" Command="{Binding CopyCommand}" />
            <Button Content="Clear" Command="{Binding ClearCommand}" />
          </StackPanel>
          <ScrollViewer Name="LogScroll" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Lines}">
              <!-- Without this the default panel is a non-virtualizing StackPanel, which
                   realizes every one of the up-to-2000 capped lines as live controls, and every
                   trim at the cap removes index 0 and re-arranges every remaining child — the
                   only O(n)-per-line cost in the whole logging chain. -->
              <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  <VirtualizingStackPanel />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
              <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate>
                  <!-- The MESSAGE is selectable; the time and the code are not. Selection is per
                       control, so no drag can span two of these columns anyway, and a
                       SelectableTextBlock is not free: measured under Skia over 40 lines, all three
                       columns selectable cost 27.3ms a frame against 15.4ms for plain TextBlocks,
                       while the message alone costs 21.4ms. The message is where the URLs and the
                       error text are; the other two are what the Copy button is for.

                       Cross-line selection is not on offer either way — one joined string in a
                       read-only TextBox would buy it at the cost of the per-line severity colour
                       and of virtualization over the 2000-line cap.

                       The styles this carries are written ":is(TextBlock)" in Controls.axaml. A
                       bare "TextBlock" selector is an EXACT type match in Avalonia and reaches no
                       SelectableTextBlock, silently: the classes stay in the markup, so the sweeps
                       that check class coverage stay satisfied while the line loses its family, its
                       size and its colour. -->
                  <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="6">
                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="log-line secondary" Text="{Binding Time}" />
                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Classes="log-line secondary" Text="{Binding Code}" />
                    <SelectableTextBlock Grid.Column="2" Classes="log-line" Text="{Binding Text}"
                                         Classes.log-warning="{Binding IsWarning}"
                                         Classes.log-error="{Binding IsError}"
                                         TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                  </Grid>
                </DataTemplate>
              </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            </ItemsControl>
          </ScrollViewer>
        </DockPanel>
      </Border>
    </Grid>

  </DockPanel>
</Window>
